    "source": "# Part 7 | Use one-way ANOVA as a feature-screening tool\n\n## What question does ANOVA answer?\n\nOne-way ANOVA helps us investigate whether a **categorical feature** is associated with a **numerical target**.\n\nFor example:\n\n> Is the average engagement score different across programmes?\n\n### Hypotheses\n\n- **Null hypothesis:** the mean engagement score is the same across all programmes.\n- **Alternative hypothesis:** at least one programme has a different mean engagement score.\n\nUsing an alpha level of `0.05`:\n\n- `p < 0.05`: the feature shows evidence of a relationship with engagement score\n- `p >= 0.05`: there is not enough evidence of different group means\n\nANOVA is a **screening tool**. It does not prove causation or guarantee that the feature will improve the machine learning model."
  },
  {
    "cell_type": "markdown",
    "metadata": {},
    "source": "## Step 11: Run a one-way ANOVA test\n\nWe will test `programme` when it is available. Otherwise, the notebook will use `region`.\n\nThe code:\n\n1. combines the categorical feature with the training target\n2. creates one engagement-score group for each category\n3. removes groups with fewer than five records\n4. runs `stats.f_oneway()`\n5. prints a practical interpretation"
  },
  {
    "cell_type": "code",
    "execution_count": null,
    "metadata": {},
    "outputs": [],
    "source": "if 'programme' in X_train_raw.columns:\n    GROUP_COLUMN = 'programme'\nelif 'region' in X_train_raw.columns:\n    GROUP_COLUMN = 'region'\nelse:\n    GROUP_COLUMN = None\n\n\nif GROUP_COLUMN is not None:\n    anova_data = pd.DataFrame(\n        {\n            GROUP_COLUMN: X_train_raw[GROUP_COLUMN],\n            TARGET_COLUMN: y_train,\n        }\n    ).dropna()\n\n    groups = [\n        group[TARGET_COLUMN].to_numpy()\n        for _, group in anova_data.groupby(GROUP_COLUMN)\n        if len(group) >= 5\n    ]\n\n    if len(groups) >= 2:\n        f_statistic, p_value = stats.f_oneway(*groups)\n\n        print('Feature tested:', GROUP_COLUMN)\n        print('F-statistic:', round(f_statistic, 4))\n        print('p-value:', round(p_value, 4))\n\n        if p_value < ALPHA:\n            print(\n                f'Conclusion: {GROUP_COLUMN} shows evidence of '\n                f'different mean {TARGET_COLUMN} values.'\n            )\n            print(\n                'Action: retain it as a candidate feature and '\n                'test whether it improves the pipeline.'\n            )\n        else:\n            print(\n                f'Conclusion: there is insufficient evidence that '\n                f'mean {TARGET_COLUMN} differs across {GROUP_COLUMN}.'\n            )\n            print(\n                'Action: treat it as a weaker candidate, but do not '\n                'remove it without checking model performance.'\n            )\n    else:\n        print('ANOVA requires at least two groups with five or more records.')\nelse:\n    print('No suitable categorical feature was found for ANOVA.')"
  },
  {
    "cell_type": "markdown",
    "metadata": {},
    "source": "## How ANOVA links to feature selection\n\nANOVA gives us an initial indication of whether a categorical feature separates the target into groups with different averages.\n\nThe practical workflow is:\n\n1. **Run ANOVA** to screen a categorical feature.\n2. **Retain promising features as candidates.**\n3. **Encode the categories** inside the preprocessing pipeline.\n4. **Run automated feature selection** after preprocessing.\n5. **Compare model performance** on unseen data.\n\n> A significant ANOVA result means the feature is worth investigating.\n>\n> It does not mean the feature must be selected.\n\nThe final decision depends on whether the complete pipeline becomes more accurate, useful and maintainable."
